# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python
venv/
ENV/

# Model files (if they're too large)
*.pt
*.pth

# VS Code
.vscode/
*.code-workspace

# Logs
*.log
logs/
```

3. Add your files to Git:
```cmd
git add .
git status  # Check which files will be committed
```

4. Create your first commit:
```cmd
git commit -m "Initial commit: Food Detection with YOLOv8"
```

5. Create a new repository on GitHub:
   - Go to https://github.com/
   - Click "New" or "+" -> "New repository"
   - Name your repository (e.g., "CalorieTracker")
   - Don't initialize with README or .gitignore
   - Click "Create repository"

6. Link your local repository to GitHub and push the code:
```cmd
git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/CalorieTracker.git
git push -u origin main
```

7. If you want to include the model file (if it's under GitHub's size limit):
```cmd
git lfs install
git lfs track "*.pt"
git add .gitattributes
git commit -m "Add Git LFS tracking for model files"
git push origin main
```

Note: Replace `YOUR_USERNAME` with your actual GitHub username. If you haven't already:
- Install Git LFS if you want to push large files: https://git-lfs.com/
- Configure Git with your credentials:
```cmd
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your.email@example.com"
```

Your code should now be available on GitHub at: `https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/CalorieTracker`# filepath: c:\Users\prati\CascadeProjects\CalorieTracker_backup\CalorieTracker\.gitignore
